we have two more talks we're going to  have two more keynotes  Karen Sita will talk in just a second  and and dr. Pachauri will follow but so  a current sort of late edition but we're  fortunate have Karen Seto heroes who is  the coordinator coordinating lead author  of the IPCC chapter on human settlements  infrastructure and spatial planning this  is a new chapter in the latest IPCC  report Karen's a professor of geography  and urbanization at the at FES at the  Yale School of Forestry and  environmental studies and she's also  co-chair of the future earth project on  urbanization and global environmental  change her research is focused on urban  land use change forecasting urban growth  and environmental consequences of urban  expansion and her she specializes in  China and India where she's been working  for the past 15 years so please welcome  Karen Sita thanks mark it's nice to be  here and fresh off of the IPCC plenary  from last week I taught my class this  morning class call climate change  mitigation in urban areas and I gave  them to the up-to-date blow-by-blow what  happened last week in Berlin so anyhow  it's nice to be here to be added on last  last minute I have just a few minutes  before patchy comes up and gives the  final keynote what i thought i would do  is give a very broad overview on really  the three key messages that came out of  working group three of the IPCC and then  I will I thought I would then zoom in on  the six key messages from the new  chapter the chapter on human settlements  infrastructure and spatial planning just  a little bit of background I imagine  everyone in this room is familiar with  the IPCC reports but just to give a  little bit of background on this  particular report of working group three  it is a labor of love that involve  hundreds of scientists from around the  world the the timeline for this report  actually started in 2009 with a scoping  meeting that took place in Italy we're  about a hundred so scientists came  around from around the world to identify  the to develop the annotated outlines  for what would be the working groups one  two and three reports so really the  planning for working group three started  in 2009 but the writing itself only  began in 2011 so the report working  group 3 has 16 chapters there are 235  lead authors and coordinating lead  authors but if you add the contributing  authors as well that actually boosts  this number from 235 up to well over 500  so really it has involved a number of  authors from around the world the final  report is more than 2,000 pages and  includes almost 10,000 references and  and underwent three different rounds of  peer review that included more than  38,000 comments and yes we responded to  every single one blind by line so as in  terms of the main key messages from  working group through there are really  three messages and really they're quite  simple as well me the first message is  not a very optimistic one and that is  that global greenhouse gas emissions  continue to increase and they've been  increasing at a increasing rate over the  last decade and this is despite  increases in mitigation efforts all  around the world so what we're seeing is  that although we're seeing increases in  mitigation efforts and and mitigation  policies at the national level at the  sub-national level and also at City  levels we're seeing that emissions are  continuing to rise the majority of the  increase in emissions comes from the  combustion of fossil fuels and also from  industrial processes  the second key message from working  group three is that these emissions are  rising and they're rising along with  growth in GDP and also population growth  and here one of the key findings of the  report is that this rise in emissions  that parallel of the growth of GDP  really reverses a long-standing a trend  of decarbonisation of energy so the  second key message is also not very  optimistic the third key message is that  we really need to undertake deep cuts  now in the very short term that without  more mitigation in the very short term  it's likely that global mean surface  temperature might increase by over three  degrees c and up to almost five degrees  c over this entire century so here the  key message is that we need to do  something soon and that if we do  something sooner we would actually  reduce the risks in the long run so  those are the three key messages from  the report missions are increasing  increasing at a higher rate we're saying  that if we don't make deep cuts in a  very short term that global mean  temperature may rise between three and a  half to almost five degrees C over this  century I want to spend about five  minutes just discussing the main  messages from the new chapter on human  settlements infrastructure and spatial  planning and also give a little bit of  context about why this new chapter was  developed so in the past the IPCC  reports especially working group three  reports have focused on mitigation from  a sector perspective what what are the  mitigation options from the energy  sector from transportation from  buildings and from industry and in the  past the issue of how cities and how  human settlements can mitigate and  emissions has been seen through the lens  of these individual sectors about six  seven  years ago there was a community of us  outside of the ipcc who were not  involved with the IPCC reports and we  approached a number of co-chairs and the  IPCC and said urbanization is a major  trend it's one of the biggest trends  that's facing humanity now and there's  an opportunity to think about mitigation  through the lens of human settlements  little did I know that IPCC would  actually take us up on this and so in  2009 with the scoping meeting in Italy  it was decided that there would be this  new chapter on how human settlements  would have the potential to mitigate  emissions so this chapter had to  coordinating and lead authors the other  coordinating lead author is Shobha card  to call who was out of Thailand we had  30 authors including 17 contributing  authors we have we received more than  3,000 comments there was a lot of  interest in this chapter from  governments from around the world and  our chapter alone has has almost 700  references so we have six key messages  from this chapter and the first message  is that urbanization is a global trend  and that around the world urbanization  is associated with increases in income  and that higher urban incomes are  generally correlated with higher  consumption of energy and greenhouse gas  emissions and here it's important to  point out that there are stark  differences between annex 1 and annex  two countries here on the left we have a  graph of total final consumption per  capita energy for an x1 countries and  what we see here is that in annex 1  countries urban emissions per capita and  also urban energy per capita are  generally lower than national averages  that is urban areas gender Benezet NCR  consuming less energy emitting less  emissions per capita than national  averages but we see a very different  and in non-annex one countries and in  non-annex one countries we see that  urban emissions and per capita urban  emissions and per capita energy  generally is above national averages and  this is not surprising because basically  what we're seeing is that in non nx1  countries the cities are the  manufacturing hubs that are producing  the products that we here in annex 1  countries are buying so this explains  why we see these differences in per  capita urban emissions in annex 1 verses  non annex 1 countries now the second key  message from our chapter I would say is  a message of hope and that is that the  next two decades presents a very unique  window of opportunity to mitigate  emissions in urban areas largely because  a large portion of the world's urban  areas the building's the transport  systems the energy systems have yet to  be built over the next three to four  decades we the most recent UN  projections suggest that we're going to  see somewhere between 2 to 4 billion  more people living in cities in urban  areas around the world and what we're  seeing is that over these next two to  three decades where we're going to see a  lot of new development in roads and  buildings and in energy systems and so  the opportunity here is to avoid the  infrastructure lock in to avoid the  behavior lock in that we see in high  energy consuming society so here I want  to really emphasize that this is really  a message of hope and and really an  opportunity that we really need a season  in the short term the third key message  from our chapter is that while  mitigation options really vary by by  country by by history by development  patterns and urbanization trajectories  what we found in the chapter and the  assessment is that urban or urban  mitigation strategies are most likely to  be effective when policy instruments are  bundled together and here we have a  diagram of  you're from the chapter which shows on  the first column some of the key  elements of low-carbon cities that would  for example have high levels of job  density relatively high levels of  residential density and at the same time  low carbon settlements and low carbon  urban areas will be ones that are  connected where destinations are  relatively accessible by a number of  different origins so the key here is to  bundle a number of policy instruments  together to not focus on just sector  level individual buildings or just  transport but to really think about  integrated land-use transport and urban  planning as the mitigation strategy one  of the things that we found in the  assessment is that while the next few  decades presents this window of  opportunity this window of opportunity  is going to occur largely in developing  countries and it is in the same  developing countries where there are  limited governance institutions  technical and very importantly really  limited financial capacities and here  there's a lot of discussion in the in  among the the chapter authors and also  across the different chapters on key  challenges that are around financing  mitigation strategies going into the  future now one thing that I think we've  seen here in North America and also in  Europe is that thousands of cities  around the world are implementing  climate change action plans we here in  New Haven I know there's a discussion  about a climate change action plan New  York's climate action plan is probably  among the most well-known in in the  United States what we found was that  despite thousands of cities undertaking  these climate action plans their  aggregate impacts on reducing emissions  is highly uncertain and the reason for  this is a multifold one is  that there are not standardized  reporting standards reporting mechanisms  there are a number of very ambitious  targets but again very unclear  accounting mechanisms and reporting  mechanisms and so although there are a  lot of cities around the world that are  undertaking these plans and implementing  them it's unclear what their cumulative  impacts are in the future what they will  be and then finally in terms of how  cities can implement these climate  action plans and also large scale or  urban scale mitigation what we found is  that for cities and for localities to be  effective with mitigation really  requires that mitigation action and  mitigation efforts be a linked with  local local co-benefits in other words  cities around the world especially those  in developing countries are faced with a  number of different challenges whether  these challenges are provision of clean  water energy sanitation education there  are a lot of other pressing immediate  challenges that cities around the world  face and the key message here is that  climate change mitigation strategies  that the local level are likely to be  ineffective unless they can be linked or  paired with these pressing concerns that  cities are facing around the world and  here I think this is also a message of  hope and another window of opportunity  that as cities around the world and  countries around the world are investing  billions of dollars in new  infrastructure in building the cities of  tomorrow there is an opportunity to  think strategically about building  cities that are also low carbon  settlements the chapter highlights a  number of knowledge gaps in the science  literature I list them here I'm not  going to read through them I think for  the students who are in the room this is  good news for you because if you're  interested in studying  urbanization or climate change or  mitigation of climate change in urban  areas this is a very fruitful area of  research that should yield decades of us  of science so with that I will close and  I think I have time for just a couple of  questions thank you
 

